#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199047} :activity/id #uuid "f34a1f9e-dc25-5a56-b69f-2cc175647229" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050747 :topic/id #uuid "6358ff23-2fc9-5b14-8d25-ca07cea2fd02" :topic/math-academy-id 4302} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199047 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199524 :tutorial/content "Logical operations with predicates generalize the corresponding operations on statements. As a result, all the properties of these operations are held.\n\nIn particular, for predicates $P(x_1,x_2\\ldots,x_n)$ and $Q(y_1,y_2\\ldots,y_m),$ De Morgan's laws state the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(P∧Q) & ≡¬P∨¬Q \\\\ ¬(P∨Q) & ≡¬P∧¬Q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese can be used to negate open sentences containing conjunctions and disjunctions. For example, let's negate the following:\n\n$$\nn \\ge 0 \\:\\textbf{ or }\\: 2 \\mid m\n$$\n\nApplying De Morgan's law for disjunctions, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(n\\ge 0\\,\\text{ or }\\,2∣m) & ≡¬(n\\ge 0∨2∣m) \\\\  & ≡¬(n\\ge 0)∧¬(2∣m) \\\\  & ≡n<0∧2∤m \\\\  & ≡n<0\\,\\text{ and }\\,2∤m.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "476f8b41-cb95-5623-94e3-dc7a863c3df5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7990 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d42da4d8-71de-5617-a193-de6538fc1c98" :step/math-academy-id 143820 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199048}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199048 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198256 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198113 :question/id #uuid "4402b53e-3c80-5788-b727-20941348c03f" :question/math-academy-id "e-12885"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "391d581f-9a2a-5de6-afbe-897917a9f6e3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048378} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049170} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049546}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198695} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198696} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414636} {:db/id #edb/ref 175921864